  "verbalization": "Zopiclone, Clonazepam, and Nitrazepam are all sedative-hypnotic drugs that share a common mechanism of action involving the binding of their compounds to Gabrg2, a protein involved in neuronal signaling. The evidence suggests that Zopiclone causes alcohol intoxication or poisoning, which is then mediated by Clonazepam or Nitrazepam, both of which bind to Gabrg2. This indicates a potential pathway where Zopiclone may indirectly influence Gabrg2 through its effects on the central nervous system, possibly via the activation of Clonazepam or Nitrazepam. The shared ancestry of these drugs with Organonitrogen Heterocyclic Compounds and their association with Sedative And Hypnotic effects further supports a unified mechanism involving Gabrg2. The integration of these paths highlights a possible role for Gabrg2 in the pharmacological effects of these sedative-hypnotic agents, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
